# PPPoE Load Balancer Examples
# ============================
#
# This directory contains example configurations for the ng_pppoe_lb
# (PPPoE Load Balancer) netgraph node and pppoed daemon.
#
# Each configuration file is extensively documented with:
#   - What it does and why you'd use it
#   - Architecture diagrams
#   - Expected outputs
#   - Step-by-step verification
#   - Troubleshooting tips
#
# For detailed documentation, see: ng_pppoe_lb(4)

# =============================================================================
# QUICK START
# =============================================================================

# Step 1: Load kernel modules
kldload netgraph
kldload ng_ether
kldload ng_pppoe
kldload ng_pppoe_lb

# Step 2: Choose a configuration (see below)
# Step 3: Run the configuration: ngctl -f <config-file>
# Step 4: Start the daemon: pppoed -L -w N em0

# =============================================================================
# CONFIGURATION FILES
# =============================================================================

# basic_2workers
# -------------
# WHAT: Creates a load balancer with 2 worker nodes
# WHY:  Testing, dual-core systems, low session counts
# START: 2 workers (fixed)
# AUTO-SCALING: No
# CAPACITY: ~1000 sessions
#
# Use when:
#   - First time testing the load balancer
#   - System has only 2 CPU cores
#   - Session count is expected to be low (<500)
#
# Try this first!

# basic_4workers
# --------------
# WHAT: Creates a load balancer with 4 worker nodes
# WHY:  Production on quad-core, moderate-high session counts
# START: 4 workers (fixed)
# AUTO-SCALING: No
# CAPACITY: ~2000 sessions
#
# Use when:
#   - Production deployment on quad-core system
#   - Predictable, constant load
#   - Want consistent distribution without scaling overhead
#
# Recommended for most production deployments

# governor_auto
# -------------
# WHAT: Load balancer with automatic worker scaling based on CPU
# WHY:  Variable load, "set and forget" operation
# START: 2 workers (auto-scales up to 4)
# AUTO-SCALING: Yes (CPU-based)
# CAPACITY: 500-2000 sessions (scales with load)
#
# Use when:
#   - Load varies significantly (evening/weekend peaks)
#   - Want automatic resource optimization
#   - Can't predict session counts in advance
#
# Governor scales workers up when CPU > 80%
# Governor scales workers down when CPU < 20%

# monitor_example
# ---------------
# WHAT: Commands for monitoring the load balancer
# WHY:  Health checks, troubleshooting, capacity planning
#
# Contains:
#   - Status checks
#   - Statistics commands
#   - Continuous monitoring
#   - Health check script
#   - Troubleshooting guide

# =============================================================================
# SHELL COMPLETIONS
# =============================================================================

# Bash completions (requires bash-completion port):
sudo cp _ngctl_pppoe_lb /usr/local/etc/bash_completion.d/
sudo cp _pppoed /usr/local/etc/bash_completion.d/
exec bash  # Reload bash to activate completions

# Tcsh completions (FreeBSD default shell):
cp ngctl.completion ~/.cshrc
cp pppoed.completion ~/.cshrc
source ~/.cshrc

# =============================================================================
# STARTUP CONFIGURATION (/etc/rc.conf)
# =============================================================================

# Add to /etc/rc.conf for automatic startup:

# Option 1: Basic 4-worker setup
#pppoed_enable="YES"
#pppoed_interface="em0"
#pppoed_flags="-L -w 4"

# Option 2: Auto-scaling with governor
#pppoed_enable="YES"
#pppoed_interface="em0"
#pppoed_flags="-L -w 2 -G 0 -M 2"

# See share/examples/etc/rc.conf.pppoe_lb for more options

# =============================================================================
# AUTO-SCALING GOVERNMENT
# =============================================================================

# The governor monitors CPU usage and automatically adjusts workers:

# pppoed with full governor options:
pppoed -L -w 2 -G 0 -M 2 -I 5 -c 80 -C 30 em0

# Option reference:
#   -L              Enable load balancer mode
#   -w 2            Start with 2 workers
#   -G 0            Max workers (0=auto-detect CPU cores)
#   -M 2            Minimum workers (never scales below)
#   -I 5            Governor poll interval (seconds)
#   -c 80           Scale up when CPU > 80%
#   -C 30           Scale down when CPU < 30%

# Governor parameters can also be set via sysctl:
#   net.graph.pppoe_lb.governor.enabled=1
#   net.graph.pppoe_lb.governor.cpu_threshold=80
#   net.graph.pppoe_lb.governor.cpu_low_threshold=30

# =============================================================================
# VERIFICATION
# =============================================================================

# After starting the load balancer, verify it's working:

# 1. Check nodes exist:
ngctl list | grep pppoe

# 2. Check worker count:
sysctl net.graph.pppoe_lb.governor.current_workers

# 3. Check session count:
sysctl net.graph.pppoe_lb.governor.total_sessions

# 4. Monitor continuously:
watch -n 5 'sysctl net.graph.pppoe_lb.governor | grep -E "(workers|sessions)"'

# =============================================================================
# TESTING
# =============================================================================

# For comprehensive integration tests (run in VM only):
#   cd tests/netgraph
#   sudo ./ng_pppoe_lb_vm_test.sh

# =============================================================================
# TROUBLESHOOTING
# =============================================================================

# Problem: "No such file or directory" when running ngctl
# Fix:    kldload ng_pppoe_lb

# Problem: Governor not scaling
# Fix:    sysctl net.graph.pppoe_lb.governor.enabled=1

# Problem: Sessions not distributed evenly
# Fix:    Check worker states with: sysctl net.graph.pppoe_lb.workers

# Problem: High error count
# Fix:    Check hardware, cables, switches

# See monitor_example for detailed troubleshooting commands

